sult in severe injuries or fatalities if not properly managed, and fluid leaks, explosions and unexpected movements of components all pose significant safety risks.","risk":"Safety hazards inherent to hydraulics"},{"note":"Hydraulic systems use fluids to transmit power; leaks can cause fluid contamination and spillage with negative environmental consequences, and hydraulic fluids can be harmful to the environment and water sources if not handled and disposed of properly.","risk":"Fluid leakage and environmental impact"},{"note":"Hydraulic systems require regular maintenance and inspection; neglect can lead to equipment failures, unexpected downtime and safety hazards.","risk":"Maintenance and inspection dependency"},{"note":"Pumps, valves and hoses wear over time under high pressures and forces, leading to inefficiencies, reduced performance and increased risk of system failure.","risk":"Component wear and tear"},{"note":"Hydraulic systems have historically been criticised for energy inefficiency (fluid leaks, internal friction, pressure losses); as environmental concerns and energy costs rise, demand shifts toward more energy-efficient hydraulic solutions, pressuring legacy product lines.","risk":"Energy inefficiency of hydraulic technology"},{"note":"Prevailing global uncertainty and the continuous reorganisation of supply chains, both in India and other countries, has the potential to impact the Company's revenues.","risk":"Geopolitical impacts and supply-chain reorganisation"},{"note":"Technologies are ever evolving, creating risk of changing preferences in pumping technologies, novel technologies displacing existing ones, and increasing competition for existing markets especially from copycat products.","risk":"Technology obsolescence and copycat competition"},{"note":"MD&A and the Board's Report refer to 'the manufacturing unit' in the singular and state there was no change in the nature of business; the Company operates in a single niche hydraulics business, 